1
00:00:00,261 --> 00:00:02,005
This video starts something new:

2
00:00:02,508 --> 00:00:03,431
random variables.

3
00:00:04,113 --> 00:00:06,319
Here's a die from a board game, rolled

4
00:00:06,461 --> 00:00:07,303
two hundred times.

5
00:00:07,906 --> 00:00:09,693
A four came up thirty-eight times.

6
00:00:10,497 --> 00:00:13,064
Let's watch all two hundred rolls land, and

7
00:00:13,165 --> 00:00:14,247
fill in the other faces.

8
00:00:15,092 --> 00:00:17,126
Four came up more than any other face.

9
00:00:17,773 --> 00:00:19,126
So is the die fair?

10
00:00:20,054 --> 00:00:21,717
Before we can answer, we need to know

11
00:00:21,799 --> 00:00:23,743
what the counts from a fair die should

12
00:00:23,764 --> 00:00:24,124
look like.

13
00:00:24,848 --> 00:00:26,935
That's what a probability distribution tells us.

14
00:00:27,638 --> 00:00:30,273
So how do data, and a model, each

15
00:00:30,314 --> 00:00:31,198
give us a distribution?

16
00:00:31,815 --> 00:00:33,924
First, a name for the number we're recording.

17
00:00:34,568 --> 00:00:36,717
A random variable is a number decided by

18
00:00:36,758 --> 00:00:38,585
the outcome of a chance experiment.

19
00:00:39,250 --> 00:00:39,511
Here,

20
00:00:40,076 --> 00:00:41,767
X is the number on the top face.

21
00:00:42,492 --> 00:00:44,619
It's discrete, which means its values are separate,

22
00:00:44,881 --> 00:00:47,048
and we can list them: one, two, three,

23
00:00:47,210 --> 00:00:48,554
four, five and six.

24
00:00:49,337 --> 00:00:51,320
We write P of X equals four for

25
00:00:51,341 --> 00:00:53,646
the probability that X takes the value four.

26
00:00:54,469 --> 00:00:56,939
Capital X is the variable, and small x

27
00:00:57,081 --> 00:00:58,627
stands for any one of its values.

28
00:00:59,453 --> 00:01:01,184
When it's clear which variable we mean, we

29
00:01:01,205 --> 00:01:02,110
just write P of x.

30
00:01:02,916 --> 00:01:05,250
Now turn the counts into probabilities.

31
00:01:05,915 --> 00:01:08,187
Divide each count by the number of rolls,

32
00:01:08,751 --> 00:01:09,213
two hundred.

33
00:01:10,018 --> 00:01:12,204
Thirty-eight out of two hundred is zero point

34
00:01:12,365 --> 00:01:12,846
one nine.

35
00:01:13,449 --> 00:01:15,094
That fraction of the rolls is called a

36
00:01:15,316 --> 00:01:16,318
relative frequency.

37
00:01:16,964 --> 00:01:17,084
Now

38
00:01:17,590 --> 00:01:18,215
watch the bars.

39
00:01:19,002 --> 00:01:20,849
Every bar is divided by the same number,

40
00:01:21,172 --> 00:01:23,441
so the shape doesn't change, only the scale.

41
00:01:24,105 --> 00:01:27,216
These numbers are estimates, so we write approximately

42
00:01:27,397 --> 00:01:28,922
equals, not equals.

43
00:01:29,647 --> 00:01:32,183
A relative frequency describes these two hundred rolls.

44
00:01:32,707 --> 00:01:34,935
The probability belongs to the die itself.

45
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:38,006
Replay the rolls and watch the estimate for

46
00:01:38,027 --> 00:01:38,468
a four.

47
00:01:38,991 --> 00:01:40,978
After ten rolls it read zero point four.

48
00:01:41,501 --> 00:01:43,689
After fifty, it was already near zero point

49
00:01:43,731 --> 00:01:43,930
two.

50
00:01:44,595 --> 00:01:46,670
More rolls usually give a better estimate.

51
00:01:47,396 --> 00:01:49,988
Add the six unrounded estimates and you get

52
00:01:50,150 --> 00:01:50,812
exactly one.

53
00:01:51,878 --> 00:01:54,006
But adding them only shows that it happens.

54
00:01:54,489 --> 00:01:55,897
Here's why it has to happen.

55
00:01:56,783 --> 00:01:59,258
Every roll lands on exactly one face.

56
00:01:59,923 --> 00:02:02,090
No roll is counted twice, and none is

57
00:02:02,151 --> 00:02:02,391
missed.

58
00:02:02,995 --> 00:02:05,000
So the six counts add to two hundred,

59
00:02:05,342 --> 00:02:06,404
the total number of rolls.

60
00:02:07,088 --> 00:02:09,174
Divide that total by two hundred, and you

61
00:02:09,215 --> 00:02:09,656
get one.

62
00:02:10,438 --> 00:02:14,060
That works for a complete, one-value-per-trial count, so

63
00:02:14,281 --> 00:02:16,962
unrounded estimates from data always add to one.

64
00:02:17,704 --> 00:02:18,910
Rounded ones can be a little off.

65
00:02:19,668 --> 00:02:21,977
What we've built is a probability distribution.

66
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:24,304
It's the set of values X can take,

67
00:02:24,665 --> 00:02:26,750
together with the probability of each value.

68
00:02:27,335 --> 00:02:28,185
We show it two ways.

69
00:02:28,814 --> 00:02:30,888
A table gives the exact numbers we calculate

70
00:02:30,930 --> 00:02:31,070
with.

71
00:02:31,615 --> 00:02:33,110
A bar graph shows the shape.

72
00:02:33,717 --> 00:02:35,908
The bars stand apart, because X takes no

73
00:02:35,949 --> 00:02:36,733
values in between.

74
00:02:37,618 --> 00:02:40,204
Every probability must be between zero and one,

75
00:02:40,646 --> 00:02:42,370
and together they must add to one.

76
00:02:42,936 --> 00:02:43,728
Both checks matter.

77
00:02:44,233 --> 00:02:46,537
These three numbers add to one, but minus

78
00:02:46,618 --> 00:02:48,942
zero point four can't be a probability, so

79
00:02:49,123 --> 00:02:50,505
this list isn't a distribution.

80
00:02:51,276 --> 00:02:53,552
Data is one way to get a distribution.

81
00:02:54,117 --> 00:02:55,200
A model is the other:

82
00:02:55,824 --> 00:02:57,852
reasoning about how the experiment works.

83
00:02:58,576 --> 00:03:00,821
If the die is fair, its six faces

84
00:03:01,003 --> 00:03:04,331
are equally likely, so each value has probability

85
00:03:04,492 --> 00:03:06,476
one sixth, before a single roll.

86
00:03:07,340 --> 00:03:09,450
So in a question, if you're given counts,

87
00:03:09,753 --> 00:03:10,918
you're estimating from data.

88
00:03:11,422 --> 00:03:13,728
If you're told how the experiment works, you're

89
00:03:13,789 --> 00:03:14,430
using a model.

90
00:03:15,175 --> 00:03:15,857
Now compare.

91
00:03:16,643 --> 00:03:19,119
The data gave zero point one nine for

92
00:03:19,140 --> 00:03:19,461
a four.

93
00:03:20,086 --> 00:03:22,577
The model says one sixth, about zero point

94
00:03:22,658 --> 00:03:23,281
one six seven.

95
00:03:24,065 --> 00:03:26,398
That's almost five more fours than a fair

96
00:03:26,419 --> 00:03:27,142
die would average.

97
00:03:27,789 --> 00:03:28,521
Is that a big gap?

98
00:03:29,429 --> 00:03:31,758
Here are three runs of two hundred rolls

99
00:03:31,819 --> 00:03:33,344
from a simulated fair die.

100
00:03:33,988 --> 00:03:35,276
Look how much their bars wobble.

101
00:03:36,101 --> 00:03:38,289
In each of these fair runs, some face

102
00:03:38,430 --> 00:03:40,618
is further from thirty-three than our four is.

103
00:03:41,261 --> 00:03:43,488
So a gap this size happens easily with

104
00:03:43,529 --> 00:03:44,090
a fair die.

105
00:03:44,874 --> 00:03:46,800
That's an illustration, not a formal test,

106
00:03:47,323 --> 00:03:49,269
but these data aren't strong evidence that the

107
00:03:49,310 --> 00:03:50,072
die is unfair.

108
00:03:50,648 --> 00:03:53,959
Often there's no data, just a description, and

109
00:03:54,001 --> 00:03:55,867
we generate the distribution ourselves.

110
00:03:56,711 --> 00:03:59,202
Toss a fair coin three times, and let

111
00:03:59,364 --> 00:04:00,489
X be the number of heads.

112
00:04:01,253 --> 00:04:03,860
Each sequence of tosses gives exactly one value

113
00:04:03,941 --> 00:04:04,361
of X.

114
00:04:05,125 --> 00:04:06,912
Step one, list the values.

115
00:04:07,555 --> 00:04:10,082
X can be zero, one, two or three.

116
00:04:10,927 --> 00:04:12,903
Step two, find each probability.

117
00:04:13,427 --> 00:04:16,291
There are eight equally likely sequences, and three

118
00:04:16,312 --> 00:04:18,796
of them have exactly one head, so P

119
00:04:18,837 --> 00:04:20,278
of one is three eighths.

120
00:04:21,163 --> 00:04:21,584
Step three,

121
00:04:22,088 --> 00:04:22,309
check.

122
00:04:23,093 --> 00:04:25,660
One eighth, three eighths, three eighths and one

123
00:04:25,741 --> 00:04:28,608
eighth are each between zero and one, and

124
00:04:28,650 --> 00:04:29,391
they add to one.

125
00:04:30,194 --> 00:04:32,222
The graph shows something the table hides.

126
00:04:32,765 --> 00:04:35,276
It's symmetric, and the middle values are three

127
00:04:35,338 --> 00:04:36,864
times as likely as the ends.

128
00:04:37,601 --> 00:04:39,028
Here's a way to remember why it all

129
00:04:39,169 --> 00:04:39,792
adds to one.

130
00:04:40,659 --> 00:04:41,224
One trial,

131
00:04:41,749 --> 00:04:42,193
one value.

132
00:04:43,216 --> 00:04:46,159
Every trial gives exactly one value of X,

133
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:48,181
so the counts add to the number of

134
00:04:48,261 --> 00:04:50,844
trials, and the probabilities add to one.

135
00:04:51,706 --> 00:04:53,591
If your probabilities don't add to one,

136
00:04:54,153 --> 00:04:56,179
check your list of values, and your counting.

137
00:04:58,863 --> 00:04:59,306
Your turn.

138
00:04:59,991 --> 00:05:02,360
A spinner numbered one to four is spun

139
00:05:02,681 --> 00:05:03,826
two hundred and fifty times.

140
00:05:04,628 --> 00:05:06,911
Estimate P of X equals two,

141
00:05:07,532 --> 00:05:09,916
then explain why the four estimates add to

142
00:05:10,156 --> 00:05:10,256
one.

143
00:05:10,939 --> 00:05:11,814
Pause here to answer.

144
00:05:22,327 --> 00:05:24,588
Two came up seventy times out of two

145
00:05:24,649 --> 00:05:27,630
hundred and fifty, so P of X equals

146
00:05:27,751 --> 00:05:28,030
two

147
00:05:28,572 --> 00:05:30,772
is approximately zero point two eight.

148
00:05:31,735 --> 00:05:33,702
For the reason, don't just add the four

149
00:05:33,823 --> 00:05:34,204
estimates.

150
00:05:34,886 --> 00:05:37,828
Each spin lands on exactly one number, so

151
00:05:37,869 --> 00:05:39,689
the counts add to two hundred and fifty,

152
00:05:40,231 --> 00:05:42,291
and dividing by two hundred and fifty gives

153
00:05:42,593 --> 00:05:42,712
one.

154
00:05:43,554 --> 00:05:45,439
Adding the estimates to get one shows it,

155
00:05:45,801 --> 00:05:47,285
but it doesn't explain it.

156
00:05:48,981 --> 00:05:49,368
Last one.

157
00:05:49,895 --> 00:05:52,098
A bag holds two red and two yellow

158
00:05:52,159 --> 00:05:54,141
counters, and two are drawn together.

159
00:05:54,823 --> 00:05:57,747
Find the probability distribution of X, the number

160
00:05:57,768 --> 00:05:58,769
of red counters drawn.

161
00:05:59,576 --> 00:06:00,368
Pause here to answer.

162
00:06:11,156 --> 00:06:13,002
X can be zero, one or two.

163
00:06:13,826 --> 00:06:16,733
Call the counters red one, red two, yellow

164
00:06:16,915 --> 00:06:17,957
one and yellow two.

165
00:06:18,620 --> 00:06:19,964
There are six possible pairs,

166
00:06:20,507 --> 00:06:21,590
all equally likely.

167
00:06:22,434 --> 00:06:24,718
One pair has no reds, four pairs have

168
00:06:24,899 --> 00:06:27,324
one red, and one pair has two reds.

169
00:06:28,067 --> 00:06:30,799
So the probabilities are one sixth, four sixths

170
00:06:31,021 --> 00:06:32,948
and one sixth, and they add to one.

171
00:06:33,897 --> 00:06:35,107
One third each is wrong.

172
00:06:35,633 --> 00:06:37,830
Three values don't have to be equally likely.

173
00:06:39,440 --> 00:06:42,706
So data give a distribution through relative frequencies,

174
00:06:43,187 --> 00:06:45,551
and a model gives it by reasoning about

175
00:06:45,631 --> 00:06:46,913
how the experiment works,

176
00:06:47,414 --> 00:06:49,197
here with equally likely outcomes.

177
00:06:50,139 --> 00:06:51,207
Next, a question.

178
00:06:51,891 --> 00:06:54,339
For one fair die, all six bars are

179
00:06:54,359 --> 00:06:55,081
the same height.

180
00:06:55,723 --> 00:06:57,471
Now tear a page at random off a

181
00:06:57,551 --> 00:06:58,254
desk calendar.

182
00:06:58,878 --> 00:07:00,668
The date could be anything from the first

183
00:07:00,829 --> 00:07:01,735
to the thirty-first.

184
00:07:02,461 --> 00:07:04,095
Are those dates equally likely too?

185
00:07:06,868 --> 00:07:07,670
Here are the key notes.

186
00:07:08,314 --> 00:07:10,041
Pause the video if you need more time

187
00:07:10,103 --> 00:07:10,584
to copy them.
